Z||000000||Formula 18||01-18-2001||12:27 AM||||Anyone ever tried that TechLine Cermakote? You can buy it through Jegs. It is supposed to be do it yourself jet-hot coating that you paint on the bake for an hour or so... <P>Any one with coated manifolds? How do they perform? Is it really cooler in there? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||01-18-2001||08:46 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.techlinecoatings.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.techlinecoatings.com/</A>  they make a whole bunch of coatings. Also tells about the DIY coatings and how to prep for them.<P>I'll be checking it out in spring.... ||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||01-18-2001||02:56 PM||||A good friend used it on his V8 headers. Worked great and still on there after 2 years. Make sure the little lady don't know what you're doing with the oven.....||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||01-18-2001||08:39 PM||||Techline also has Satin Black coating which doesn't need baking. That's the one I'm going to try on my 4 cyl manifold.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||01-19-2001||12:57 AM||||Email Techline and ask for their resellers/dealer catalog- you will have to give them a business name. The prices in the dealer catalog are really good and there are lots of coatings available that the consumer catalog does not mention. My personal favorite is the black thermal shedding coating- great for alternator housings, intakes and brake calipers to help them dissipate heat.||||reg||1
